<mujoco model="xarm6_pusht_slalom">
  <!-- Push the T through a three-gate slalom: no straight line reaches the
       goal, so the object has to weave near -> far -> near while also
       completing the family's 180-degree flip.

       NOT an IsaacGym task. The other four tabletop scenes are sim_task01..04
       re-scaled; this one was added because flat MPPI solves all of them at
       0.88-0.96 SR once its temperature is tuned, so none of them separates a
       planner that reasons about the object's route from one that does not.

       WHY A SLALOM AND NOT A NARROWER GAP. shelf_gap is already the tightest
       single gate the T fits through, and tightening it further only tests
       precision. What no scene here tests is a route with more than one
       decision in it: each reversal forces the pusher to break contact,
       travel around the object and re-approach on a different face, which is
       a multi-step maneuver a single-horizon sampler cannot plan through and
       exactly what the object block's plan is supposed to supply.

           gate 1   y = +0.22   opening x in [0.15, 0.39]   (near side)
           gate 2   y =  0.00   opening x in [0.34, 0.58]   (far side)
           gate 3   y = -0.22   opening x in [0.15, 0.39]   (near side)

       THE OPENINGS ARE 0.240 m -- 2.7 T-crossbars, and 1.84x the T's 0.131 m
       greatest width in any direction, so orientation is never what fails.
       They are deliberately WIDER than shelf_gap's proven 0.200 m single
       gate: the difficulty here is meant to be the sequence, and a first
       layout at 0.200 m with 0.28 m between opening centres stalled ADMM at
       gate 2 for 13% of a 500-step run (it cleared gate 1, then could not
       make the lateral move in the band it had).

       CONSECUTIVE OPENINGS OVERLAP BY 0.050 m IN x, WHICH IS THE POINT. The
       overlap is smaller than the T's own 0.089 m minimum width, so no
       straight line through two consecutive gates exists and the weave is
       still forced -- but the excursion needed is only 0.19 m between
       opening centres rather than the full opening width. Shortest route
       ~1.00 m against the corridor's own 0.800 m.

       Each gate is two fins running to a table edge, so nothing goes round:

         * gates 1 and 3 span the near edge (x = -0.05) and the far edge
           (x = 0.75) outright.
         * gate 2's far fin reaches the far edge. Its near fin stops at
           x = 0.15 rather than the near edge because the arm is bolted
           there -- the base shell (radius 0.0912) already blocks
           x in [-0.05, 0.0912] at this y, and the 0.059 m slot left between
           the two is impassable to the 0.089 m crossbar.

       The fins are 0.04 m thick in y (against shelf_gap's 0.16 m shelves),
       which is what leaves 0.18 m of clear band between consecutive gates
       for the object to turn in -- twice the T's own 0.089 m minimum width.
       Thinner fins do not make the task easier; they buy the maneuvering
       room that keeps three gates solvable at all.

       THEY ARE ALSO HALF THE HEIGHT of every other obstacle in this family:
       0.07 m, against the 0.14 m shelf_gap and the YCB scenes use. Still
       0.0104 m proud of the 0.0596 m block, so the OBJECT is blocked exactly
       as a taller fin would block it, while halving what the STICK must
       climb to change lanes. The tip is held at the block's 0.0298 m
       mid-height and `_tip_height_cost` is quadratic in centimetres above
       it, so crossing a 0.14 m fin costs (100*0.11)^2 = 121 against a total
       near-goal task cost of order 1, and crossing a 0.07 m one costs
       (100*0.041)^2 = 17. Unlike every other scene here the arm has to cross
       fins repeatedly -- that is what a slalom IS -- so this is charged
       several times a run rather than never.

       The outermost gates sit at |y| = 0.22, inside the family's |y| <= 0.3
       obstacle band, so both ends of the corridor stay clear: the T at its
       nominal start and goal clears the nearest fin by 8.1 cm.

       oim/utils/scenes.py carries the matching Boxes; tests/test_scenes.py
       fails if the two drift apart. -->
  <include file="common.xml"/>
  <include file="tee.xml"/>
  <include file="tee_start.xml"/>

  <worldbody>
    <!-- shelf_gap's camera verbatim (elevation=-60, azimuth=180,
         distance=1.30). Kept steeper than the other tabletop scenes' -25
         even though these fins are half shelf_gap's height: the weave is
         the thing being watched, and it only reads from above. See
         shelf_gap.xml for the derivation. -->
    <camera name="front" pos="1.0310 0.0450 1.2258"
      xyaxes="0.0000 1.0000 0.0000 -0.8660 0.0000 0.5000"/>

    <geom class="tabletop_obstacle" name="fin_1_near" type="box" size="0.10 0.02 0.035" pos="0.05 0.22 0.035"/>
    <geom class="tabletop_obstacle" name="fin_1_far" type="box" size="0.18 0.02 0.035" pos="0.57 0.22 0.035"/>
    <geom class="tabletop_obstacle" name="fin_2_near" type="box" size="0.095 0.02 0.035" pos="0.245 0.0 0.035"/>
    <geom class="tabletop_obstacle" name="fin_2_far" type="box" size="0.085 0.02 0.035" pos="0.665 0.0 0.035"/>
    <geom class="tabletop_obstacle" name="fin_3_near" type="box" size="0.10 0.02 0.035" pos="0.05 -0.22 0.035"/>
    <geom class="tabletop_obstacle" name="fin_3_far" type="box" size="0.18 0.02 0.035" pos="0.57 -0.22 0.035"/>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
